questions.question:
The progress service's outbox publisher (topic progress-events, 6 partitions, the default partitioner picks the partition from a hash of the key):

await producer.send({
  topic: "progress-events",
  messages: [{ key: row.event_id, value: JSON.stringify(row.payload) }],
});

Two events of the same learner, published 40 ms apart:

event_id=7f1c-...  learner=u_17  type=progress.updated  percent=90   -> partition 4, offset 88123
event_id=a20e-...  learner=u_17  type=progress.updated  percent=100  -> partition 1, offset 90417

The progress read model has one consumer instance per partition.

Statement: "The read model can apply percent=100 before percent=90 for u_17, even though the broker keeps every partition in order."

After the fix the events are keyed by learner id. To cut the lag, a developer changes the consumer of partition 4:

consumer.run({
  eachBatch: async ({ batch }) => {
    await Promise.all(batch.messages.map((m) => applyProgress(JSON.parse(m.value))));
  },
});

Consumer log (applyProgress writes progress.percent for the learner):

14:02:07.112 start  p4 offset=88123 u_17 percent=90   (UPDATE waits for a row lock 180 ms)
14:02:07.113 start  p4 offset=88124 u_17 percent=100
14:02:07.151 done   p4 offset=88124 u_17 percent=100
14:02:07.297 done   p4 offset=88123 u_17 percent=90

Statement: "Since every message in the batch comes from one partition, Promise.all still applies each learner's events in offset order."

